    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d978">

                <pb facs="1328" n="1318" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1318"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.932/6–1345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-18T13:00:00-04:00">June 18, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>928. Department has received a letter from Soong dated June 13<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d978-f1"/> stating that he has appointed Dr. Kan Lee, Commercial
                    Counselor of Embassy, to begin preliminary discussions with us regarding
                    proposed treaty of friendship, commerce and navigation. Soong states Kan Lee
                    will return to Chungking after conversations to discuss relevant points with
                    various departments of Chinese Government concerned with treaty. This does not
                    change the plan to conduct the negotiations in Chungking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d978-f1" n="69" xml:id="d978-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it was addressed to the Assistant Secretary of State (Clayton).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
